  "ai_overview": {
    "text_blocks": [
      {
        "type": "paragraph",
        "snippet": "Yes, standard Utah homeowners policies typically cover hail damage as a named peril, minus your deductible and subject to your policy's specific terms.",
        "reference_indexes": [
          0,
          1,
          2
        ]
      },
      {
        "type": "heading",
        "snippet": "What Is Typically Covered"
      },
      {
        "type": "list",
        "list": [
          {
            "snippet": "Roofing: Cracked, dented, or missing shingles, plus damage to underlayment and roof vents.",
            "reference_indexes": [
              1,
              3
            ]
          },
          {
            "snippet": "Exterior & Windows: Dents or cracks in siding, gutters, downspouts, skylights, and window glass.",
            "reference_indexes": [
              1,
              3
            ]
          },
          {
            "snippet": "Interior & Belongings: Water damage inside if hail breaks your roof or windows (personal property coverage may also apply to damaged indoor items).",
            "reference_indexes": [
              1
            ]
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "type": "heading",
        "snippet": "Common Exclusions & Cost Factors"
      },
      {
        "type": "list",
        "list": [
          {
            "snippet": "Deductibles: Your payout is reduced by your deductible, which may be a flat dollar amount or a percentage of your dwelling coverage (no Utah law sets a mandatory percentage deductible).",
            "reference_indexes": [
              0,
              2,
              4
            ]
          },
          {
            "snippet": "Roof Age & Depreciation: Older roofs may be settled on an actual cash value (depreciated) basis rather than full replacement cost, depending on endorsements.",
            "reference_indexes": [
              0,
              3
            ]
          },
          {
            "snippet": "Cosmetic Exclusions: Some policies limit or exclude cosmetic-only denting (such as on metal roofing or gutters).",
            "reference_indexes": [
              0,
              3
            ]
          },
          {
            "snippet": "Flooding: Storm runoff or flooding caused by heavy rain or hail is not covered under a standard homeowners policy and requires separate flood insurance.",
            "reference_indexes": [
              5,
              6
            ]
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "type": "heading",
        "snippet": "Utah Claim Rules to Know"
      },
      {
        "type": "list",
        "list": [
          {
            "snippet": "No Deductible Rebates: Under Utah law (Utah Code 13-50), residential contractors cannot rebate or discount your deductible on storm repairs.",
            "reference_indexes": [
              3
            ]
          },
          {
            "snippet": "Claim Assignments: Utah law restricts signing over claim benefits to contractors and requires specific disclosures; a 2026 law also allows policies to block assigning a claim to a roofer (though not to a policy adjuster).",
            "reference_indexes": [
              0,
              3
            ]
          },
          {
            "snippet": "Nonrenewal Protection: A single unavoidable wind, hail, or lightning claim generally cannot be the sole reason for nonrenewal if it is your only such claim in 36 months.",
            "reference_indexes": [
              0
            ]
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "type": "paragraph",
        "snippet": "For official guidance on filing and handling claims in the state, see the Utah Insurance Department.",
        "snippet_links": [
          {
            "text": "Utah Insurance Department",
            "link": "https://insurance.utah.gov/consumers/home-insurance/home-claims-info/"
          }
        ],
        "reference_indexes": [
          5
        ]
      },
      {
        "type": "paragraph",
        "snippet": "If you share whether you are dealing with recent storm damage or shopping for a policy\u2014along with your deductible type and approximate roof age\u2014I can help you weigh next steps or estimate out-of-pocket costs."
      }
    ],
